  "slug": "imaging-photosynthetic-living-plants-1563",
  "summary": "Five preprints pair antiporter and PsbS genetics with in vivo pH and metabolite biosensors to resolve how thylakoid proton dynamics tune NPQ.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is how proton and ion fluxes across the thylakoid membrane shape photosynthetic regulation, especially energy-dependent quenching (qE). In Arabidopsis, PsbS mutants with altered lumenal protonation sites show a small zeaxanthin- and acidification-linked quenching, implying complexity in PsbS protonation. NHD1, a thylakoid Na+/H+ antiporter, supports acclimation to fluctuating light and partly overlaps functionally with KEA3 and VCCN1. In Chlamydomonas, KEA3 modulates qE relaxation, including in darkness, and affects growth and low-CO2-induced protein accumulation. New is the pairing of these genetic dissections with in vivo readouts: pHluorin2 was selected among four biosensors to image stromal and lumen pH, and biosensors were combined with chlorophyll fluorescence to link stromal and cytosolic metabolism with photosynthetic function.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
